revision.text:
'''Akagi Shrine''' (赤城神社) is a {{ill|Shinto|ja|神道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto|qq=}} shrine located in {{ill|Maebashi|ja|前橋市|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Maebashi|qq=}}, {{ill|Gunma Prefecture|ja|群馬県|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gunma Prefecture|qq=}}, Japan. It is a {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|qq=}} ({{ill|Myōjin Taisha|ja|名神大社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin Taisha|qq=}}) and is also regarded as a potential site of the Second Shrine of {{ill|Kōzuke Province|ja|上野国|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōzuke Province|qq=}}. It was formerly designated as a Prefectural Shrine under the pre-World War II Modern Shrine Ranking System.

It is officially called "Akagi Shrine," but to distinguish it from other shrines of the same name, it is also referred to as "'''Miyosawa Akagi Shrine'''" (三夜沢赤城神社). Among the approximately 300 Akagi shrines across the country, especially centered around the Kantō region, this shrine is considered one of the most likely to be the original main shrine.

== Overview ==
The shrine sits on the southern mountainside of {{ill|Mount Akagi|ja|赤城山|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mount Akagi|qq=}} in central Gunma Prefecture. Prior to the Meiji period, the shrine consisted of two separate shrines (East and West), but they were unified into a single entity during the Meiji era. The approach to the shrine diverges into three directions at the first torii gate: toward {{ill|Ōgo|ja|大胡町|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōgo|qq=}} in the south, Naegashima in the east, and Ichinoseki in the west. The path toward Ōgo retains a row of pine trees planted during the {{ill|Edo period|ja|江戸時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Edo period|qq=}}.

The honden (main sanctuary) and nakamon (middle gate) are designated as Important Cultural Properties by Gunma Prefecture. Approximately 500 meters south of the honden along the path is the sōmon (main gate), which is also a prefectural cultural property. Between the haiden (worship hall) and nakamon stands the "Tawara Sugi," a cedar tree designated as a Natural Monument by the prefecture. Additionally, the grounds include the "Monument of Jindai Moji," established in March 1870, a relic of {{ill|Fukko Shintō|ja|復古神道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fukko Shintō|qq=}}, and designated as an Important Cultural Property by Maebashi City.

== Worshipped deities (Saijin) ==
{{see also|{{ill|Saijin|ja|祭神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saijin|qq=}}}}
* Akagi-no-Kami
* {{ill|Toyoki-Irihiko-no-Mikoto|ja|豊城入彦命|1=Toyokiirihiko-no-Mikoto|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Toyoki-Irihiko-no-Mikoto|13=User:Immanuelle/Toyokiirihiko-no-Mikoto|qq=}}
* {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大己貴命|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōkuninushi|qq=}}

== History ==
=== Establishment ===
The shrine's establishment date is unknown. According to shrine tradition, it was founded when Toyoki-Irihiko-no-Mikoto began ruling over {{ill|Kōzuke Province|ja|上毛野国|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōzuke Province|qq=}}, accompanied by Ōkuninushi.

About 1.3 km up from the shrine lies a ritual site centered around a large rock known as {{ill|Hitsuishi|ja|櫃石|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hitsuishi|qq=}}, which indicates the presence of ancient Shinto practices.

=== General History ===
