==== Heian Period ====
In the ''{{ill|Zoku Nihon Kōki|ja|続日本後紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Zoku Nihon Kōki|qq=}}'', it is recorded that in the 6th year of the {{ill|Shōwa|ja|承和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shōwa|qq=}} era (839) the deity was conferred the Rank of Junior Fifth Rank Lower; in the ''{{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|ja|日本三代実録|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|qq=}}'' it is recorded that between the 9th and 16th years of the {{ill|Jōgan|ja|承和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jōgan|qq=}} era (867–874) the deity was promoted from Junior Fifth Rank Upper to Senior Fifth Rank Lower, and in the 4th year of the {{ill|Genkei|ja|元慶|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Genkei|qq=}} era (880) to Senior Fourth Rank Upper.<ref group="note">A record in the Nagagen period document ''Ueno Province Alternating Records'' lists the Rank as Senior First, though no promotion record exists.</ref> Later, in the medieval period, the shrine became the Second Shrine of {{ill|Ueno Province|ja|上野国|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ueno Province|qq=}}. However, debate exists as to whether the deity mentioned in these records—with a Ronsha present at another shrine—refers to Miyosawa Akagi Shrine (see {{ill|Akagi Shrine#History|ja|赤城神社#歴史|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Akagi Shrine#History|qq=}}).

In the 14th century collection of legends ''{{ill|Shinto Shu|ja|神道集|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto Shu|qq=}}'', the small pond of Mount Akagi’s crater lake, the large pond, and the central crater’s Jizodake are deified. The small pond god is associated with {{ill|Kokūzō Bosatsu|ja|虚空蔵菩薩|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kokūzō Bosatsu|qq=}}, the large pond god with {{ill|Senju Kannon|ja|千手観音|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Senju Kannon|qq=}}, and Jizodake is identified with {{ill|Jizō Bosatsu|ja|地蔵菩薩|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jizō Bosatsu|qq=}} (the latter being a later addition). According to records handed down in the West Shrine, the West Shrine enshrined Kokūzō and Senju Kannon, while the East Shrine enshrined Jizō.

According to the "Miyagi Murashi", the Miyosawa Akagi Shrine is distinct from the government shrine Akagi seen in texts such as the ''Rokukokushi''. Originally, the East Shrine was established around the late 13th to early 14th century as "Akagi Shrine" along with the flourishing of the Jizodake faith; subsequently, the shrine located in the area known as "Former Miyosawa"<ref group="note">The Miyosawa Akagi Shrine tradition states that the shrine grounds were relocated, with the eastern site known as Former Miyosawa. The Utsū Ruins discovered there have been identified as the former shrine site.</ref> relocated next to the East Shrine. This new site became the West Shrine, estimated to have been established around the 1st year of the {{ill|Jōwa|ja|貞和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jōwa|qq=}} era (circa 1345). The former Miyosawa shrine is also said to have been the mountain shrine of the Nomiya Akagi Shrine.

After the West Shrine was relocated, Miyosawa Akagi Shrine came to consist of both an East and a West Shrine, although the longstanding East Shrine was stronger.

During the Sengoku period, documents indicate that almost all references to the shrine included "Miyosawa" in the name, not recognizing it as the main shrine of Akagi. However, by the 4th year of the {{ill|Tenshō|ja|天正|1=Tenshō (Momoyama period)|lt=Tenshō Era|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tenshō|13=User:Immanuelle/Tenshō (Momoyama period)|qq=}} era (1576), after the Nomiya Akagi Shrine in Ninomiya Town was destroyed by local rivals, there were no shrines rivaling the Miyosawa Akagi Shrine and its East Shrine, and it came to be recognized as the main shrine of Akagi.<ref>According to the "Miyagi Murashi".</ref>

==== Edo Period ====
On February 20 in the 17th year of the {{ill|Keichō|1=Keichō|2=ja|3=慶長|ja|慶長|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Keichō|qq=}} era (1612), a donation from residents of Ōmaeda Village (present-day Ōmaeda-cho, Maebashi City) resulted in a row of pine trees being planted along the approach.

In the 12th year of the {{ill|Hōreki|1=Hōreki|2=ja|3=宝暦|ja|宝暦|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hōreki|qq=}} era (1762), the East Shrine was conferred Senior First Rank, followed by the West Shrine in the 2nd year of the {{ill|Meiwa|ja|明和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiwa|qq=}} era (1765).

In the 12th year of the {{ill|Kansei|ja|寛政|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kansei|qq=}} era (1800), the {{ill|Daidō Akagi Shrine|ja|大洞赤城神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Daidō Akagi Shrine|qq=}} of Maebashi’s Fujimicho, Akagi Mountain, using the title "Main Shrine", initiated litigation against Miyosawa Akagi Shrine’s attendant temple, Jujōji. In the 2nd year of the {{ill|Kyōwa|ja|享和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kyōwa|qq=}} era (1802), the Miyosawa side appealed to the shogunate’s Temple and Shrine Magistrate, but the matter was resolved by returning it to state jurisdiction. The litigation was prolonged, and an agreement regarding the use of names was reached in the {{ill|Bunka|ja|文化 (元号)|ja|文久|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Bunka|qq=}} 13th year (1816).

==== From the Meiji Period Onwards ====
In the 2nd year of the {{ill|Meiji|ja|明治|1=Meiji era|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji era|qq=}} era (1869), due to the policy of abolishing Buddhism, two shrine-temples—Ryūseki-ji of the East Shrine and Shinkō-ji of the West Shrine—were closed. Thereafter, the two shrines of Miyosawa Akagi Shrine, formerly East and West, merged into a single shrine. Although documents from the 2nd year of the {{ill|Kyōwa|ja|享和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kyōwa|qq=}} era (1802) already treated it as a single shrine, it was officially designated as such in the early Meiji period. However, as the shrine’s records do not mention the merger, the exact timing remains unknown. No buildings were erected at the former West Shrine site, and at the East Shrine site the current shrine buildings (the main hall and the middle gate) were constructed. A record exists of a topping-out ceremony held on November 25 (old calendar) in Meiji 2 (1869). In 1894 (Meiji 27), the worship hall burned down and was later rebuilt.

Under the modern shrine Ranking system, it was initially a {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|qq=}}, later elevated to a {{ill|Prefectural Shrine|ja|県社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Prefectural Shrine|qq=}}. In 1935 (Shōwa 10), a movement for elevation to a {{ill|Kokuhēisha|ja|国幣社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kokuhēisha|qq=}} arose, and in 1944 (Shōwa 19) a provisional decision for {{ill|Kokuhēichūsha|ja|国幣中社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kokuhēichūsha|qq=}} was issued; however, during the process the end of the war and directives from the GHQ led to the abolition of the shrine Ranking system, leaving it as a Prefectural Shrine.

=== Shrine Ranks ===
* Records in the ''{{ill|Rokukokushi|ja|六国史|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Rokukokushi|qq=}}'' record the conferral of shrine Ranks for "Akagi God" (though interpretations vary beyond this shrine):
** In the 6th year of the {{ill|Shōwa|ja|承和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shōwa|qq=}} era (839), in June, he was conferred Junior Fifth Rank Lower (''{{ill|Zoku Nihon Kōki|ja|続日本後紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Zoku Nihon Kōki|qq=}}'')
** In the 9th year of the {{ill|Jōgan|ja|承和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jōgan|qq=}} era (867), on June 20, he was promoted from Junior Fifth Rank Upper to Senior Fifth Rank Lower (''{{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|ja|日本三代実録|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|qq=}}'')
** On December 25 of the 11th year of the {{ill|Jōgan|ja|貞観|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jōgan|qq=}} era (869), he was conferred Senior Fifth Rank Upper (''Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku'')
** On March 14 of the 16th year of the {{ill|Jōgan|ja|貞観|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jōgan|qq=}} era (874), he was conferred Junior Fourth Rank Lower (''Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku'')
** On May 25 of the 4th year of the {{ill|Genkei|ja|元慶|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Genkei|qq=}} era (880), he was conferred Junior Fourth Rank Upper – with the inscription reading either "Akagi Ishi-no-Kami" or "Akagi Numano-Kami"<ref group="note">Manuscript variations exist between "Akagi Ishi-no-Kami" and "Akagi Numano-Kami".</ref>
* After the Rokukokushi period:
** Around the 3rd year of the {{ill|Chōgen|ja|長元|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chōgen|qq=}} era (circa 1030), he was conferred Senior First Rank (as per the ''Ueno Province Alternating Records'')
** Senior First Rank (as per the ''Ueno Province Shrine Name Records'') – recorded as "Akagi Daimyōjin"
* 18th Century:
** In the 12th year of the {{ill|Hōreki|1=Hōreki|2=ja|3=宝暦|ja|宝暦|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hōreki|qq=}} era (1762), the East Shrine received Senior First Rank
** In the 2nd year of the {{ill|Meiwa|ja|明和|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiwa|qq=}} era (1765), the West Shrine received Senior First Rank

== Shrine Facilities ==
=== Shrine Buildings ===
[[File:Akagi-jinja (Miyosawa) honden.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Main Hall (Gunma Prefecture-designated Cultural Property)}}]]
The main hall is constructed in the {{ill|Shinmei-zukuri|ja|神明造|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinmei-zukuri|qq=}} style with three bays on the front and two on the sides, in a gabled roof design with copper plate roofing. The chigi (roof finials) are internally tapered, with eight support beams (katsugi) installed. It is said, along with the middle gate, to have been built in 1869 (Meiji 2)<ref name="rekimachi">{{Cite web|title=III Akagi Mountain Faith and Historical Scenery of the Southern Foot Settlements|url=https://www.city.maebashi.gunma.jp/material/files/group/59/rekimachi_2shou_3.pdf|website=Maebashi City|accessdate=2025-02-04}}</ref>. Its construction reflects the influences of the then prevalent Shinto-Buddhism separation and restoration of ancient Shinto, and together with the middle gate it is designated as a tangible cultural property of Gunma Prefecture<ref name="Main Hall and Middle Gate">[http://www2.wagamachi-guide.com/gunma/map.asp?gpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&gscl=16000&ssiz=500,500&sid=5026&galay=0000000000000100&id=01034&insgpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&accept=939820130608102329 Miyosawa Akagi Shrine Main Hall and Middle Gate] (Gunma Prefecture Cultural Property Information System).</ref>

Within the main hall is an inner palace enshrined as the divine seat (kamiza). An inscription behind the door states "Gifted by Minenari Shige Yorin", and it is attributed to the offering by the lord of {{ill|Nintenkimazono Castle|ja|新田金山城|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nintenkimazono Castle|qq=}}, Minenari Shige Yorin. Constructed in a wooden hōkei style (not “square-style”) with a plank roof and a height of 117 cm, featuring chimaki-adorned pillars and lattice doors reminiscent of Zen style, it is characteristic of the Muromachi period and designated as a tangible cultural property of Gunma Prefecture<ref>[http://www2.wagamachi-guide.com/gunma/map.asp?gpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&gscl=16000&ssiz=500,500&sid=5026&galay=0000000000000100&id=01033&insgpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&accept=939820130608102329 Miyosawa Akagi Shrine Inner Palace] (Gunma Prefecture Cultural Property Information System).</ref>

The middle gate, a four-legged gate standing in front of the main hall, is a gabled roof structure with copper roofing, also said to have been built in 1869 (Meiji 2)<ref name="rekimachi"/><ref name="Main Hall and Middle Gate"/>.
The inner palace within the main hall, as well as the main hall and middle gate, were designated as important cultural properties of Gunma Prefecture in 1963 (Showa 38) and 1973 (Showa 48) respectively<ref name="rekimachi"/>.
