[[File:Akagi-jinja (Miyosawa) torii.JPG|thumb|250px|right|{{center|Grand Torii}}]]
'''Akagi Shrine''' (赤城神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|qid=Q845945|lt=shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} located in Miyosawa-cho, {{ill|Maebashi|en|Maebashi|qid=Q201613|lt=Maebashi City|1=Maebashi}}, {{ill|Gunma Prefecture|en|Gunma Prefecture|qid=Q129499|lt=Gunma Prefecture|1=Gunma Prefecture}}.
It is a {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|1=Shikinaisha|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|Meishin Taisha|ja|名神大社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meishin Taisha|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Myōjin Taisha|qid=Q9610964}}) {{ill|Ronsha|ja|論社|1=candidate shrines|2=ja|3=論社|lt=candidate|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ronsha|13=User:Immanuelle/candidate shrines|1=Engishiki_Jinmyōchō#Ronsha|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki_Jinmyocho|lt=Ronsha}}}}, as well as a Nomiya Ronsha of {{ill|Kōzuke Province|en|Kōzuke Province|qid=Q1054838|lt=Ueno Province|1=Kōzuke Province}}, and formerly a {{ill|Prefectural Shrine|ja|県社|1=Ken-sha|qid=Q90139226}}{{efn|Ken-sha|qid=Q90139226|lt=Ken-sha|1=modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Prefectural Shrine|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q712534}}.

Although its official name is "Akagi Shrine", to distinguish it from other Akagi Shrines it is also called "'''Miyosawa Akagi Shrine''' (みよさわあかぎじんじゃ)". It is one of the principal shrines, presumed to be the main shrine among approximately 300 Akagi Shrines across the country, mainly in the {{ill|Kantō region|en|Kantō region|qid=Q132480|lt=Kanto region|1=Kantō region}}.

== Overview ==
Located on the southern hillside of {{ill|Mount Akagi|en|Mount Akagi|qid=Q415251|lt=Mount Akagi|1=Mount Akagi}} in central {{ill|Gunma Prefecture|en|Gunma Prefecture|qid=Q129499|lt=Gunma Prefecture|1=Gunma Prefecture}}, the shrine was once regarded as having two shrines (East and West) before the Meiji era, but later became a single shrine. The approach splits into three directions: one with the first torii leading south toward the {{ill|Grand Torii|ja|大鳥居|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Grand Torii|1=Grand Torii|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q122998727}} and the Ōogomachi area, one east toward Naegashima, and one west toward Ichinoseki. The road continuing in the Ōogomachi direction still retains a pine avenue planted during the Edo period.

Both the main hall and the middle gate are designated as important cultural properties by the prefecture. Along the approach, about 500 meters south of the main hall, stands the Soumon (main gate), also designated as an important cultural property. Additionally, between the worship hall and the middle gate, immediately in front of the middle gate, is the "Tawara Cedar", a natural monument designated by Gunma Prefecture. The shrine grounds also include a "Jindai Moji Stele" erected in March 1870 (Meiji 3), regarded as an artifact of the restoration of ancient Shinto, and designated as an important cultural property by the city.

