=== Around the Shrine Buildings ===
[[File:Akagi-jinja (Miyosawa) tawara-sugi.JPG|thumb|180px|right|{{center|Tawara Cedar (Gunma Prefecture-designated Natural Monument)}}{{small|Middle Gate is at lower left.}}]]
The "Tawara Cedar" refers to three large cedar trees standing immediately to the south of the middle gate and its western neighbor. The two trees in front of the main hall stand side by side. According to legend, they were planted as offerings by {{ill|Fujiwara no Hidesato|en|Fujiwara no Hidesato|qid=Q1136623|lt=Fujiwara no Hidesato|1=Fujiwara no Hidesato}} (also known as Tawara Tōta) during his journey to the Ueno government office to subdue {{ill|Taira no Masakado|1=Taira no Masakado|2=en|3=Taira no Masakado|4=ja|5=平将門|6=de|7=Taira no Masakado|8=es|9=Taira no Masakado|10=fr|11=Taira no Masakado|lt=Taira no Masakado|qid=Q1207652|en|Taira no Masakado|1=Taira no Masakado|2=en|3=Taira no Masakado|4=ja|5=平将門|6=de|7=Taira no Masakado|8=es|9=Taira no Masakado|10=fr|11=Taira no Masakado|lt=Taira no Masakado|qid=Q1207652|qid=Q1207652|lt=Taira no Masakado}}. They have been designated as a natural monument by Gunma Prefecture<ref>[http://www2.wagamachi-guide.com/gunma/map.asp?gpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&gscl=16000&ssiz=500,500&sid=5026&galay=0000000000000100&id=01037&insgpos=139.181267133333,36.4813478449848&accept=939820130608102329 Miyosawa Akagi Shrine Tawara Cedar] (Gunma Prefecture Cultural Property Information System).</ref> A similar legend is recounted for the cedar at {{ill|Nukisaki Shrine|en|Nukisaki Shrine|qid=Q10863796|lt=Ichinomiya Nukemae Shrine|1=Nukisaki Shrine}}, suggesting the aspirations of medieval warriors towards honor<ref name="Explanatory Board">On-site explanatory board.</ref>. Many other large cedar trees also stand within the shrine grounds.

Also present is the "Jindai Moji Stele", an inscribed stone believed to bear characters from before the introduction of Chinese script, erected in March of Meiji 3 as an artifact of ancient Shinto. The inscription was written by the adopted son of {{ill|Hirata Kanetane|en|Hirata Kanetane|qid=Q6537446|lt=Hirata Atsutane|1=Hirata Kanetane}}, with the divine text composed by his son, En'in. The divine text "Manahitokoronarukowe yoshi" is written in the style of the Abiru script passed down within the Abiru family of Tsushima<ref name="Explanatory Board"/>.
Additionally, about 200 m east of the shrine grounds along the road to the Hitsuishi, there is a beech tree designated as a natural monument by the city, and a treasure pagoda (Akagi Tower) designated as a city-important cultural property.
