=== Within Mount Akagi ===
[[File:Akagi-jinja (Miyosawa) hituishi.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Hitsuishi (Gunma Prefecture-designated Historic Site)}}]]
A ritual site centered on a massive stone known as "Hitsuishi" is located about 1.3 km up Mount Akagi from the shrine grounds ({{Coord|36|29|38.43|N|139|10|30.42|E|region:JP-10_type:landmark|name=Hitsuishi}}). It measures approximately 4.7 m in length, 2.7 m in width, 2.8 m in height, with a circumference of 12.2 m. Numerous ritual objects have been found in the vicinity, and it was designated as a historic site by Gunma Prefecture in 1963 (Showa 38)<ref name="rekimachi"/><ref>[http://www2.wagamachi-guide.com/gunma/map.asp?gpos=139.178167933333,36.4909051337386&gscl=16000&ssiz=500,500&sid=5026&galay=0000000000000100&id=01036&insgpos=139.178167933333,36.4909051337386&accept=939820130608102329 Hitsuishi] (Gunma Prefecture Cultural Property Information System).</ref><ref>[http://www.gunmaibun.org/cgi-bin/iseki/database.cgi?cmd=dp&num=23369&dp= Hitsuishi] (Gunma Prefecture Buried Cultural Property Survey Group).</ref>

=== Approach (Sandō) ===
[[File:Akagi-jinja (Miyosawa) soumon.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Soumon (Gunma Prefecture-designated Cultural Property)}}]]
The approach from the Soumon to National Route 353 is lined with numerous pine trees, preserved as an Edo-period pine avenue. According to the "Akagi Shrine Chronicle", in the 17th year of the {{ill|Keichō|en|Keichō|qid=Q1328185|lt=Keichō|1=Keichō}} era (1612) approximately 1,200 pine trees were planted along the approach by donation from Hikobe of Ōmaeda Village (present-day Ōmaeda-cho, Maebashi City), each estimated to be around 400 years old. In addition, about 30,000 rhododendron plants were also planted, making the area famous in spring<ref>[http://akagi-yama.jp/archives/330 Miyosawa Akagi Shrine Pine Avenue and Rhododendron Group] (Akagi Mountain Portal Site).</ref>

The Soumon is a Takamado-mon built during the Edo period (exact date unknown). It is located about 450 m south of the shrine grounds ({{Coord|36|28|49.61|N|139|10|39.36|E|region:JP-10_type:landmark|name=Soumon}}). Although the "Akagi Shrine Chronicle" records its construction in the 1st year of the {{ill|Hōreki|en|Hōreki|qid=Q1194397|lt=Hōreki|1=Hōreki}} era (1751), some older materials are also evident. As few Takamado-mon from the early modern period exist in Gunma Prefecture, it was designated as a tangible cultural property in 1978 (Showa 53)<ref name="rekimachi"/><ref>[http://www2.wagamachi-guide.com/gunma/map.asp?gpos=139.181267133333%2C36.4813478449848&gscl=16000&ssiz=500%2C500&sid=5026&galay=0000000000000010&insgpos=139.181267133333%2C36.4813478449848&accept=939820130608102329&Map.x=246&Map.y=352 Akagi Shrine Soumon] (Gunma Prefecture Cultural Property Information System).</ref>
