== Festivals ==
* Shihō Haijinja and New Year Festival ({{ill|January 1|en|January 1|qid=Q2150|lt=January 1|1=January 1}})<ref>The festival events are recorded in the shrine’s tradition.</ref>
* Shūsei-e ({{ill|January 5|en|January 5|qid=Q2202|lt=January 5|1=January 5}})
* Tsutsugayu Sacred Event ({{ill|January 15|en|January 15|qid=Q2260|lt=January 15|1=January 15}})
* Nenki Festival ({{ill|February 19|en|February 19|qid=Q2340|lt=February 19|1=February 19}})
* Gochin Festival (afternoon in March)
* Sacred Procession (early April) – A mikoshi parade from Nomiya Akagi Shrine to Miyosawa.
* Annual Festival ({{ill|May 5|en|May 5|qid=Q2550|lt=May 5|1=May 5}})
* Gochin Festival (afternoon in November)
* Sacred Procession (early December) – A mikoshi parade from Nomiya Akagi Shrine to Miyosawa.
* New Rice Thanksgiving Festival (Shinjō-sai) ({{ill|December 10|en|December 10|qid=Q2302|lt=December 10|1=December 10}})

== Cultural Properties ==
=== Important Artifacts (National Designation) ===
* Jadeite magatama (excavated near the Akagi Shrine Hitsuishi) – Designated on July 13, 1939 (Showa 14)<ref>'Catalog of Important Artifacts' (Shibunkaku, 1972), Volume 1, p.30</ref><ref>The Maebashi City official website gives the designation date as "July 30, Showa 14", but the official gazette date is July 13 of the same year.</ref>

=== Gunma Prefecture-designated Cultural Properties ===
* Tangible Cultural Properties
** Inner Palace of the Main Hall – Designated on September 4, 1963 (Showa 38).
** Main Hall and Middle Gate – Designated on April 25, 1973 (Showa 48).
** Ancient Documents – 45 documents from Eiroku 3 (1560) to Keichō 18 (1613). Designated on April 25, Showa 48.
** Soumon – Designated on August 25, Showa 53.
* Historic Sites
** Hitsuishi – Designated on September 4, 1963 (Showa 38), with an additional designation on June 25, 1985 (Showa 60).
* Natural Monuments
** The Tawara Cedar of Miyosawa Akagi Shrine – Designated on April 25, 1973 (Showa 48).

=== Maebashi City-designated Cultural Properties ===
* Important Cultural Properties (Tangible)
** Jindai Moji Stele in the shrine grounds – Designated on April 1, 1978 (Showa 53).

** Treasure Pagoda (Akagi Tower) – Designated on April 1, 1978 (Showa 53).
* Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties
** Miyosawa Akagi Shrine Taida Kagura – 13 troupes are offered on January 1, January 5, and May 5. Designated on July 4, Showa 60.
* Natural Monuments
** The Beech of Miyosawa – Designated on April 1, 1980 (Showa 55).

== Related Locations ==
* Former Miyosawa (Murozawa, Kasukawa-cho, Maebashi City)
: Considered to be the old shrine site of the West Shrine prior to its relocation. Foundation stones, presumed to date from the Heian period, have been discovered<ref>'Place Names of Gunma Prefecture' – Murozawa Section, Kasukawa Village, Setagaya District.</ref>
* Utsū Ruins ({{ill|Utsū Ruins|ja|宇通遺跡|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Utsū Ruins|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}) (Nakanosawa, Kasukawa-cho, Maebashi City, {{ウィキ座標|36|29|20.11|N|139|12|02.23|E|region:JP-10_type:landmark|name=Utsū Ruins}})
: Ruins located in a forest in the upper reaches of the Kasukawa River. Discovered during a forest fire in 1965 (Showa 40), investigations were partial but identified foundation stone structures suggesting the remains of a temple from the syncretic period. It is speculated to be the former location of Akagi Shrine, and its relation to the multi-storied pagoda on the mountain (in {{ill|Kiryū, Gunma|en|Kiryū, Gunma|qid=Q634022|lt=Kiryū City|1=Kiryū, Gunma}}, built in the 20th year of {{ill|Enryaku|en|Enryaku|qid=Q1373176|lt=Enryaku|1=Enryaku}} (801)) has been noted<ref>'Place Names of Gunma Prefecture' – Utsū Ruins Section, Kasukawa Village, Setagaya District.</ref><ref>[http://www.gunmaibun.org/cgi-bin/iseki/database.cgi?cmd=dp&num=22585&dp= Utsū Ruins] (Gunma Prefecture Buried Cultural Property Survey Group).</ref>

